/* Curated plain-English MD-102 vocabulary: educational explanations, not official Microsoft text. */
window.MD102_V10_LAYERS=[{"id":"hardware","short":"01 · Inside a device","title":"Start inside the computer","subtitle":"From a chip and memory to a manageable endpoint","why":"You cannot decide how to manage a device until you know what is doing the work inside it.","tiny":"A processor executes instructions. Working memory holds active information. Persistent storage keeps files. Firmware starts the machine, and the OS makes its parts usable.","macro":"In an organization, endpoint administrators care about platform, OS support, TPM, storage, device ownership and whether management can reliably change settings.","story":"A new Windows laptop arrives at Northstar. Before adding policy, IT must confirm OS compatibility, supported hardware and whether its disk can be encrypted.","flow":["Power and firmware begin startup","Operating system loads and controls resources","Network and management services start","IT checks hardware prerequisites and OS health"],"question":"Why can two enrolled laptops respond differently to the exact same security policy?","answer":"Hardware and OS capabilities can differ, so support and device state must be checked.","route":"devices","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","quiz":[{"q":"Which component directly executes machine instructions?","a":["Operating system","CPU","DHCP server","Intune policy"],"correct":1,"why":"The CPU executes instructions; the OS coordinates use of hardware."},{"q":"A TPM is present but a drive is still readable without protection. What conclusion is safest?","a":["TPM alone never proves the drive is encrypted","TPM automatically turns on BitLocker","The device is definitely compliant","The device must be joined"],"correct":0,"why":"TPM protects keys; BitLocker or another encryption mechanism must actually protect the disk."}]},{"id":"network","short":"02 · Moving information","title":"How a device reaches the cloud","subtitle":"IP, DHCP, DNS, TLS, certificates, and blocked traffic","why":"Policies cannot reach a device that cannot communicate with the identity and management services.","tiny":"A network adapter exchanges packets. DHCP may supply addressing; DNS locates services; TLS protects many connections; routers, proxies and firewalls shape the path.","macro":"Intune depends on working cloud connectivity, device-side management components and service endpoints. A failure at any link may present as an enrollment or sync problem.","story":"A laptop can join office Wi-Fi but still fails enrollment. IT must determine whether its IP address, DNS, proxy, certificate trust or service sign-in is broken.","flow":["Device obtains an IP address and DNS settings","DNS resolves service names","HTTPS/TLS connects to cloud endpoints","The management service exchanges policies and status"],"question":"Why is “connected to Wi-Fi” not enough evidence that Intune enrollment will work?","answer":"Wi-Fi can work even when DNS, routing, TLS, proxy or service authentication fails.","route":"enrollment","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","quiz":[{"q":"A device can reach a service by IP, but cannot find the service by its hostname. What do you check first?","a":["Windows Hello","DNS","App detection rule","BitLocker recovery"],"correct":1,"why":"DNS maps names to resource records such as service IP addresses."},{"q":"What is the main security role of TLS in an HTTPS session?","a":["Assign a license","Enroll a device","Authenticate the server and protect traffic in transit","Create a dynamic group"],"correct":2,"why":"TLS provides connection protection after negotiating trusted cryptographic parameters."}]},{"id":"identity","short":"03 · Who and what","title":"Identity, proof, permissions and ownership","subtitle":"From passwords to Entra, tenant and joined vs registered","why":"Your first security question is not “which policy?” but “who or what is asking, and what should they be allowed to do?”","tiny":"An identity is an object. A credential proves control. Authentication checks identity. Authorization determines access. Device identity is separate from user identity.","macro":"An organization’s Entra tenant holds users, device objects and groups. Licenses enable services, while roles and policies govern the permitted operations.","story":"An employee adds their work account to a personal laptop. The user may authenticate and the laptop may register, but that does not automatically make it a managed corporate device.","flow":["User/device identity exists in directory","Credentials and factors prove identity","Tokens and permissions govern actions","Registration, join and enrollment each serve distinct roles"],"question":"What exactly is the difference between authentication and authorization?","answer":"Authentication establishes who the requester is; authorization determines what that identity may access or change.","route":"groups","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","quiz":[{"q":"An administrator signs in successfully but cannot edit a policy. Which type of control is most directly involved?","a":["Authorization / RBAC","DHCP","Storage encryption","Windows Update"],"correct":0,"why":"The identity was authenticated, but the role may not authorize that administrative action."},{"q":"A BYOD laptop has a Microsoft Entra device record. Does that prove it is Intune-enrolled?","a":["Yes, all registered devices are enrolled","Yes, when MFA is enabled","No; device registration and enrollment are distinct","Only if the laptop has a TPM"],"correct":2,"why":"An Entra device identity can exist without an Intune MDM management relationship."}]},{"id":"management","short":"04 · Managing at scale","title":"From one device to 200 managed endpoints","subtitle":"Enrollment, MDM, MAM, policy, targeting and check-in","why":"An organization needs repeatable settings and accurate evidence, not 200 manual laptop changes.","tiny":"A saved policy object describes intent. An assignment chooses eligible recipients. Enrollment establishes a management channel. Device check-in processes supported instructions.","macro":"Intune coordinates policies and applications across fleets while Entra provides identities and groups. Reporting and device-side evaluation close the feedback loop.","story":"IT creates a configuration profile, assigns it to pilot Windows laptops, then synchronizes one device. Only observed settings and matching evaluation results can establish success.","flow":["Create a policy with supported settings","Select eligible user/device targets","Device checks in and evaluates instructions","Inspect device result and remediate failures"],"question":"Why does creating a policy and clicking Save not prove that it changed a device?","answer":"A device must be eligible, reachable, enrolled, compatible and must evaluate the instructions before the state can change.","route":"configuration","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","quiz":[{"q":"Which Intune concept decides who receives an existing configuration profile?","a":["App detection rule","Assignment","TLS certificate","CPU architecture"],"correct":1,"why":"The policy assignment targets selected users/devices, sometimes refined by filters."},{"q":"A device has not checked in since a new profile was assigned. What is the most defensible status?","a":["It is definitely configured","It must be compliant","The assignment may be pending; inspect check-in and device state","The policy must be deleted"],"correct":2,"why":"A saved assignment is not proof of device-side application."}]},{"id":"security","short":"05 · Decide what is safe","title":"Settings, health checks and access are separate","subtitle":"Configuration versus compliance versus Conditional Access","why":"You must know whether a control configures a device, checks the device, or controls access to resources.","tiny":"Encryption protects stored data. Compliance evaluates device requirements. Access policy uses identity and device signals. Security telemetry shows possible attacks.","macro":"At enterprise scale, IT configures secure devices, checks health, and allows or blocks cloud-resource access through Entra. Defender and reporting reveal threats or drift.","story":"Northstar wants to protect cloud email. IT configures BitLocker, evaluates encryption through compliance, and uses Entra Conditional Access to require compliance for email access.","flow":["Configuration policy attempts to turn on encryption","Compliance evaluates required device security","Entra evaluates Conditional Access at sign-in","Access is granted or challenged/blocked; reports log why"],"question":"Which policy *turns on* BitLocker, which *checks* encryption, and which may *block email access*?","answer":"Disk-encryption configuration enables supported BitLocker settings; compliance evaluates; Entra Conditional Access gates access.","route":"compliance","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","quiz":[{"q":"Which policy normally evaluates whether a device meets requirements such as encryption?","a":["Compliance policy","Windows Autopilot profile","App detection rule","DNS record"],"correct":0,"why":"Compliance policies primarily assess required device health/security conditions."},{"q":"Which service makes Conditional Access decisions for Microsoft 365 sign-in?","a":["Windows firmware","Microsoft Entra ID","A DHCP server","The Intune Settings Catalog"],"correct":1,"why":"Conditional Access is an Entra access-control capability that can consume Intune compliance signals."}]},{"id":"deployment","short":"06 · Getting work-ready","title":"New devices, Windows updates and Cloud PCs","subtitle":"Why Autopilot is a sequence, not one button","why":"Reliable onboarding and maintenance require staged prerequisites and observable outcomes.","tiny":"Provisioning performs ordered operations: create identity, establish management, download policy, install required apps and confirm readiness.","macro":"Organizations standardize setup across teams, update devices in pilots, and monitor service or user impacts rather than sending manual instructions to everyone.","story":"A new employee receives a sealed laptop. Windows OOBE starts, Autopilot identifies the organization, enrollment occurs, ESP tracks required work, and the user reaches a configured desktop.","flow":["Device starts Out-of-Box Experience","Autopilot provides organizational setup","Join, enrollment, apps and ESP process","IT confirms actual readiness; updates continue later"],"question":"Why does assigning an Autopilot profile not guarantee that Office will be installed correctly?","answer":"Autopilot orchestrates provisioning, but application packaging, eligibility, installation and detection must also work.","route":"autopilot","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","quiz":[{"q":"What is the role of the Enrollment Status Page during supported Autopilot deployment?","a":["Replace antivirus","Track and possibly gate required setup work","Provide public IP addresses","Generate a Windows license"],"correct":1,"why":"ESP surfaces and can block until selected enrollment and setup tasks finish."},{"q":"Which update usually moves Windows to a different feature release?","a":["A quality update","A DNS update","A feature update","A license assignment"],"correct":2,"why":"Feature updates target a release/version; quality updates generally deliver ongoing security and reliability fixes."}]},{"id":"apps","short":"07 · Apps and work data","title":"Getting software onto a device—and making it safe","subtitle":"Application packaging, detection, assignment and BYOD data protection","why":"A successful deployment needs a usable app, not merely a package stored in a portal.","tiny":"Installation modifies device files and registry state. A detection rule checks what actually exists. App protection limits actions on organizational data inside supported apps.","macro":"Intune can coordinate required and available apps, dependencies and updates, while MAM protects data on both enrolled and some unenrolled devices.","story":"Finance needs an older Win32 payroll app and employees need Outlook on BYOD phones. The payroll app needs packaging and detection; Outlook needs app protection rather than broad personal-device control.","flow":["Select app type and deployment intent","Package/install with correct prerequisites","Detection confirms installed state","Protect organizational data and monitor outcomes"],"question":"Why are application deployment and app-protection policies separate?","answer":"Deployment installs or makes software available; app protection governs corporate-data handling within supported apps.","route":"apps","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/apps/app-management","quiz":[{"q":"A Win32 installer succeeds but Intune reports the app as not detected. Where should you look?","a":["DHCP option","Detection rule","Entra device join type only","Windows update ring"],"correct":1,"why":"A detection rule evaluates whether the expected installed state exists."},{"q":"The company wants to protect Outlook data on a personal phone without enrolling the whole device. Which approach is a starting point?","a":["Company-wide wipe","Win32 package","Supported app protection (MAM)","Firmware reset"],"correct":2,"why":"MAM app protection can safeguard supported work apps without enrolling the entire personal device."}]},{"id":"operations","short":"08 · Evidence and scale","title":"Prove the outcome, then automate responsibly","subtitle":"Logs, PowerShell, Graph, KQL, remediation and analytics","why":"An administrator must tell the difference between a requested action, a queued action, a completed action, and a verified result.","tiny":"Scripts carry ordered commands. APIs carry authenticated requests. Events and logs record behavior. Query languages locate relevant evidence.","macro":"At scale, teams automate repetitive safe work, detect deviations, audit changes, and report device experience and risk.","story":"IT sees 40 laptops missing an update. Instead of blindly restarting the fleet, it identifies eligibility, checks logs, pilots a fix, monitors results and documents exceptions.","flow":["Define expected outcome and success criteria","Collect logs, telemetry and current state","Apply a safe pilot fix or automation","Re-evaluate devices and document evidence"],"question":"Why is a script exit code 0 not always proof that a business issue has been solved?","answer":"A script can exit successfully while the required external device state or user-visible behavior is still wrong.","route":"reports","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","quiz":[{"q":"Which language is commonly used to analyze supported event datasets with filtering and aggregation?","a":["DHCP","KQL","BitLocker","Autopilot"],"correct":1,"why":"KQL is a query language used in supported reporting and security telemetry experiences."},{"q":"You ran remediation on 100 devices. What is the strongest evidence of success?","a":["The script was saved","The admin has a license","The dashboard opened","Eligible endpoints show the intended state after the operation"],"correct":3,"why":"A successful action request or process exit is not enough; confirm the real intended result."}]}];
window.MD102_V10_TERMS=[{"id":"endpoint","layer":"hardware","name":"Endpoint","plain":"A computer, phone, tablet, or other device where a user or workload interacts with a service.","does":"It runs apps, stores or processes data, and generates events that administrators need to manage.","why":"An organization must know which endpoints hold or access its information.","when":"When protecting laptops, BYOD phones, shared kiosks, or Cloud PCs.","how":"Identify the platform, ownership, user, enrollment state, and risks; manage the device or protect its apps according to those facts.","under":"A physical endpoint has CPU, memory, storage, an operating system and network interfaces; a virtual endpoint has equivalent virtual components.","verify":"In Devices, compare the inventory record, management status, last check-in, user, and compliance.","mistake":"Endpoint does not mean just Windows laptop; and an inventory record does not guarantee device management.","example":"A sales employee's personal iPhone accessing Outlook is an endpoint even if it is not enrolled in Intune.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"hardware","layer":"hardware","name":"Hardware","plain":"The physical components of a computer, such as its processor, memory, drive, and network adapter.","does":"Hardware performs the operations requested by the operating system and applications.","why":"Security and deployment depend on device capabilities, not just a policy name.","when":"When checking whether encryption, TPM-backed keys, or an OS upgrade is supported.","how":"Inspect hardware inventory and prerequisites before configuring policies.","under":"CPU executes instructions; RAM holds running data; storage persists data; TPM can protect cryptographic keys; network adapter sends packets.","verify":"Compare model, OS compatibility, TPM readiness, storage, and firmware information with your deployment requirements.","mistake":"Hardware and operating system are not synonyms: hardware executes; the OS coordinates.","example":"A laptop without a supported TPM may need investigation before a security rollout.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"cpu","layer":"hardware","name":"CPU","plain":"Central Processing Unit: the chip that executes machine instructions.","does":"It processes calculations and runs operating-system and application code.","why":"Performance, architecture, and certain security capabilities matter for endpoint readiness.","when":"When assessing OS requirements, app architecture, or heavy device workloads.","how":"Check processor architecture and compatibility before Windows upgrades or app packaging.","under":"The CPU reads instructions and data from memory; privileged execution modes protect the operating system.","verify":"Find processor type and architecture in device inventory or Windows system information.","mistake":"CPU is not memory; high CPU use and low available RAM are different problems.","example":"A required application built only for x64 may not run as expected on an incompatible platform.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"ram","layer":"hardware","name":"RAM","plain":"Random Access Memory: fast working memory used while a computer runs.","does":"It temporarily holds active programs and data that the processor needs.","why":"Insufficient memory can affect the user experience and troubleshooting outcomes.","when":"When a managed app crashes, devices slow down, or upgrade prerequisites are checked.","how":"Compare active memory usage and recommended app specifications.","under":"RAM is volatile: most contents disappear after power loss; long-term files live on storage.","verify":"Inspect memory use in Task Manager and device hardware information.","mistake":"RAM is not persistent disk storage or a measure of device compliance.","example":"A laptop with many browser tabs may slow down because memory pressure causes paging.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"storage","layer":"hardware","name":"Storage","plain":"Persistent space such as an SSD that holds an operating system, applications, and files.","does":"It retains installed software and data after shutdown.","why":"Data at rest may need encryption and enough capacity for updates.","when":"When deploying Windows feature updates, apps, or BitLocker policies.","how":"Check available disk space, encryption readiness and partition requirements.","under":"The file system tracks blocks and files; encryption transforms data so reading disk contents requires the authorized key.","verify":"Examine free space, installation errors, drive health, and disk-encryption status.","mistake":"Storage encryption does not replace access controls or backups.","example":"An update can fail even if the device is enrolled because the disk lacks sufficient free space.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"operating-system-os","layer":"hardware","name":"Operating system (OS)","plain":"The foundational software, such as Windows, macOS, Android, or iOS, that manages the device.","does":"It coordinates hardware, files, permissions, network access, apps, and system services.","why":"Intune capabilities depend on the platform and OS version.","when":"Before deciding which enrollment method, compliance requirement, or policy type to use.","how":"Identify platform and build; select policies that the platform supports.","under":"The kernel controls protected resources and exposes management services; vendor APIs determine which settings an MDM can change.","verify":"Check OS version/build and supported settings in the device record and vendor documentation.","mistake":"A policy available for Windows is not automatically available for iPhone or macOS.","example":"A Windows Settings Catalog option may have no equivalent control on Android.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"firmware-and-uefi","layer":"hardware","name":"Firmware and UEFI","plain":"Low-level device software that initializes hardware before Windows or another OS starts.","does":"It helps establish the startup process and hardware configuration.","why":"Secure Boot and trusted startup can depend on firmware configuration.","when":"When troubleshooting boot protection, deployment, or Windows security prerequisites.","how":"Inspect firmware settings and update through approved vendor processes.","under":"UEFI initializes devices and can verify signed boot components before the OS kernel loads.","verify":"Check firmware version and Secure Boot state in device or system reports.","mistake":"UEFI is not a Windows app, and a management policy cannot override unsupported firmware behavior.","example":"An older laptop may need a firmware update before a security deployment.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"tpm","layer":"hardware","name":"TPM","plain":"Trusted Platform Module: security hardware or firmware that can protect cryptographic keys.","does":"It helps bind secrets to a device and supports measured or trusted startup scenarios.","why":"Stolen storage should not automatically reveal encryption keys or credentials.","when":"When configuring BitLocker, Windows Hello for Business, or device trust.","how":"Check TPM availability, health, and version before requiring TPM-backed protections.","under":"The TPM can hold or protect key material and release it under authorized conditions; it does not encrypt the entire drive on its own.","verify":"Verify TPM status and a separate BitLocker encryption status.","mistake":"TPM and BitLocker are different: TPM helps protect keys; BitLocker encrypts disk volumes.","example":"A TPM may protect the key that unlocks a BitLocker-encrypted system drive.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"kernel-and-service","layer":"hardware","name":"Kernel and service","plain":"The kernel is the OS core; a service is a background program performing an ongoing job.","does":"The kernel mediates hardware access; services can handle management check-ins, antivirus, updates, and networking.","why":"Many management changes happen in the background, not by visible screen clicks.","when":"When a device reports a delayed or failed policy application.","how":"Investigate relevant service state, event logs, connectivity, and OS compatibility.","under":"User-space processes request privileged operations through OS interfaces; background services respond to triggers and schedules.","verify":"Inspect service status, Windows event logs, and device management diagnostics.","mistake":"A console showing a policy assigned does not prove the device-side service applied it.","example":"A disconnected device cannot receive a freshly assigned configuration until it reconnects and checks in.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"network","layer":"network","name":"Network","plain":"A set of devices able to exchange information using communication links and protocols.","does":"It moves sign-in requests, policies, app packages, and telemetry between endpoints and services.","why":"Intune depends on reachable cloud endpoints and working network paths.","when":"When enrollment, policy sync, authentication or app deployment is failing.","how":"Verify Wi-Fi or Ethernet connection, name resolution, route, proxy, and required HTTPS connectivity.","under":"Data is split into packets that cross local switches, routers, and internet networks toward a destination.","verify":"Test access to approved Microsoft service endpoints and review connectivity and proxy logs.","mistake":"Having a Wi-Fi icon does not prove internet reachability or access to every required service.","example":"A laptop may join office Wi-Fi but fail Intune check-in because a proxy blocks a necessary URL.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"ip-address","layer":"network","name":"IP address","plain":"An Internet Protocol address identifies a network interface for communication on an IP network.","does":"It lets devices send packets to the correct network destination.","why":"Device communication requires working addressing and routing.","when":"When diagnosing an endpoint with no access to cloud services.","how":"Check device IP address, subnet, gateway, DNS and whether the network supports the destination.","under":"Packets carry source and destination IP addresses; routers forward packets toward their target networks.","verify":"Inspect ipconfig or system network settings, then test approved destinations.","mistake":"An IP address is not a person, login account, or device management identity.","example":"A laptop with an invalid local IP configuration cannot reach the sign-in page.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"dhcp","layer":"network","name":"DHCP","plain":"Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol, commonly used to automatically provide network configuration to devices.","does":"It typically assigns an IP address, subnet mask, gateway, and DNS server information.","why":"Administrators cannot configure every workstation address manually at scale.","when":"When many devices fail networking right after connecting to a LAN.","how":"Confirm the client received a valid lease and correct network options.","under":"A client requests addressing from a DHCP service; the server offers a temporary lease with configuration options.","verify":"Look at the DHCP lease and device network settings.","mistake":"DHCP does not resolve hostnames; DNS does.","example":"A new laptop gets a valid local address but still cannot browse because the DNS server setting is wrong.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"dns","layer":"network","name":"DNS","plain":"Domain Name System: a distributed system for converting names into records such as IP addresses.","does":"It helps an endpoint find services by name instead of memorizing numeric addresses.","why":"Intune enrollment and authentication require locating remote service endpoints.","when":"When a browser or enrollment component cannot reach a named Microsoft service.","how":"Compare DNS resolution with network reachability and proxy settings.","under":"A resolver queries DNS hierarchy or caches, receives resource records, and gives addresses to an application.","verify":"Use approved name-lookup commands and check the resolver response.","mistake":"DNS success does not guarantee the website or authentication service is functioning.","example":"A laptop can ping an IP but fail to open the company portal because a hostname cannot resolve.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"https-and-tls","layer":"network","name":"HTTPS and TLS","plain":"HTTPS is web traffic protected by Transport Layer Security (TLS).","does":"It authenticates the server and encrypts traffic in transit.","why":"Credentials, policy traffic, and administrative actions should not travel openly across networks.","when":"When signing in to Microsoft services or troubleshooting connection and certificate failures.","how":"Confirm HTTPS connectivity and a valid certificate chain; inspect proxies that perform TLS inspection.","under":"A TLS handshake negotiates cryptographic protection after certificate validation, then protects application data.","verify":"Check the browser security state, system clock, network inspection, and relevant TLS errors.","mistake":"HTTPS protects the connection, not the trustworthiness of every downloaded application.","example":"A wrong device clock can cause a certificate validation error during sign-in.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"certificate","layer":"network","name":"Certificate","plain":"A signed digital document binding a public key to an identity or service.","does":"It helps systems authenticate endpoints, users or servers and establish trusted connections.","why":"Organizations need scalable trust without sharing a secret with every server.","when":"When enabling Wi-Fi/VPN authentication, HTTPS trust, Cloud PKI, or device certificates.","how":"Identify issuer, subject, intended use, expiration and private-key custody.","under":"Certificate verification follows signatures and a chain of trust; the matching private key stays protected.","verify":"Inspect chain, expiration, subject name, and revocation/trust status as appropriate.","mistake":"A certificate is not itself the secret key and is not proof a device is compliant with policy.","example":"A VPN connection fails when the device certificate expires even though the MDM enrollment is healthy.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"vpn","layer":"network","name":"VPN","plain":"Virtual Private Network: an encrypted pathway to specific network resources over another network.","does":"It can carry approved business traffic to private services.","why":"Some internal apps are not directly reachable on the public internet.","when":"When remote users need protected access to private resources.","how":"Configure the supported VPN profile, authentication, certificates and routes.","under":"A tunnel encapsulates and protects packets; split tunneling may send only designated routes through it.","verify":"Inspect connection status, route table, DNS resolution and target app access.","mistake":"VPN connectivity is not equivalent to device identity or compliance.","example":"A managed laptop connects to corporate VPN but still fails Conditional Access for cloud email because it is noncompliant.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"proxy-and-firewall","layer":"network","name":"Proxy and firewall","plain":"A proxy intermediates selected connections; a firewall permits or blocks traffic according to rules.","does":"They shape which applications and devices can communicate and with whom.","why":"Security teams limit unnecessary network exposure and log suspicious flows.","when":"When enrollment or application downloads unexpectedly time out.","how":"Review relevant firewall rules, proxy authentication and allowed Microsoft endpoints.","under":"Traffic is evaluated by policy using destination, port, protocol, identity or application context.","verify":"Compare intended allow rules against actual connection logs and failures.","mistake":"Allowing all network traffic is not a safe default fix for an enrollment problem.","example":"A restrictive proxy blocks Microsoft update content although all policies appear assigned.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"packet-and-port","layer":"network","name":"Packet and port","plain":"A packet is a unit of network data; a port identifies a service endpoint for TCP or UDP communication.","does":"Together with IP addressing, they route application data toward a specific process.","why":"Troubleshooting requires distinguishing network path failures from app failures.","when":"When a required cloud service is unreachable or a VPN app times out.","how":"Identify the documented protocol and destination; test only approved connectivity paths.","under":"Network devices examine packet headers, while a receiving computer hands data to the application listening on that port.","verify":"Verify destination, port, protocol and any middlebox logs.","mistake":"Port 443 commonly carries HTTPS, but an open port alone does not prove authentication succeeded.","example":"A laptop establishes a TCP connection yet fails HTTPS when TLS certificate validation fails.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"identity","layer":"identity","name":"Identity","plain":"A digital representation of a user, device, application, or service.","does":"It provides a stable subject that security systems can recognize.","why":"Businesses must distinguish who or what requests access before deciding what is allowed.","when":"At sign-in, device registration, app access, and policy targeting.","how":"Create and manage accounts and objects in the correct directory; check attributes and lifecycle status.","under":"Identity systems store objects and identifiers separately from the credentials used to prove control.","verify":"Inspect the correct object and its sign-in or registration records.","mistake":"Identity is not the same as a password; a password is one possible credential.","example":"Northstar has an identity for a user and a separate identity for their managed laptop.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"groups"},{"id":"user-account","layer":"identity","name":"User account","plain":"A directory object representing a person's organizational access and attributes.","does":"It can hold memberships, license assignments and authentication settings.","why":"Permissions should follow a managed person or role, not be shared indiscriminately.","when":"When an employee joins, changes jobs, or leaves.","how":"Create the account, assign appropriate groups/licenses, configure authentication and remove access when no longer needed.","under":"The identity provider validates credentials and issues security tokens with claims for approved sessions.","verify":"Check the account status, groups, roles, license, and sign-in logs.","mistake":"An account is not automatically a mailbox, device enrollment, or paid license.","example":"A new hire can exist in Entra but cannot use an Intune-dependent feature until prerequisites are met.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"users"},{"id":"credentials","layer":"identity","name":"Credentials","plain":"Evidence used to prove control of an identity, such as passwords, passkeys, certificates or keys.","does":"They help demonstrate that a requester is entitled to act as an account.","why":"Without proof, a name typed into a sign-in form would be meaningless.","when":"Every sign-in, service-to-service connection, or privileged operation.","how":"Prefer strong approved credentials, secure storage, and MFA or phishing-resistant methods.","under":"The authentication provider verifies the credential; successful proof leads to tokens or sessions rather than giving the app the secret.","verify":"Check sign-in method and audit logs, not the user's raw password.","mistake":"A credential does not define authorization; a valid login may still lack permission.","example":"An IT user signs in successfully but cannot edit compliance policies due to missing RBAC rights.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"groups"},{"id":"authentication","layer":"identity","name":"Authentication","plain":"The process of establishing that a requester is who or what it claims to be.","does":"It checks identity proof at login or when a protected operation requires it.","why":"Organizations cannot trust an asserted username alone.","when":"Before accessing Microsoft 365, an admin center, or sensitive settings.","how":"Configure supported sign-in methods and inspect authentication requirements and failures.","under":"Entra validates credentials and required factors, then can issue identity and access tokens.","verify":"Check sign-in logs, factor requirements, result and error codes.","mistake":"Authentication answers 'who are you?'; authorization answers 'what may you do?'.","example":"A user enters the correct password but is prompted for MFA before sign-in completes.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"groups"},{"id":"authorization","layer":"identity","name":"Authorization","plain":"The decision about which actions or resources an authenticated identity may use.","does":"It enforces permissions such as reading devices but not wiping them.","why":"A successful login must not imply unrestricted administrative power.","when":"When assigning admin roles, app scopes, or access policies.","how":"Grant least-privilege roles and verify resource scope and effective permissions.","under":"Services evaluate roles, permissions, policies and claims for each operation.","verify":"Attempt the allowed action with a test account and inspect relevant audit records.","mistake":"Authorization does not fix a failed authentication; they occur at different stages.","example":"A help-desk administrator can view devices but lacks permission to delete policy objects.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"groups"},{"id":"mfa","layer":"identity","name":"MFA","plain":"Multi-factor authentication: sign-in using more than one independent type of proof.","does":"It reduces reliance on a stolen or guessed password.","why":"Password compromise alone should not provide easy access to accounts.","when":"When protecting user logins and especially privileged administrator access.","how":"Configure appropriate Entra authentication methods and access requirements.","under":"The identity provider demands an additional factor such as a possession-based authenticator before issuing access.","verify":"Inspect successful sign-in method and Conditional Access details.","mistake":"Two passwords are not two independent authentication factors.","example":"An administrator signs in with a password and an authenticator-based method.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"groups"},{"id":"microsoft-entra-id","layer":"identity","name":"Microsoft Entra ID","plain":"Microsoft's cloud identity and access service, formerly Azure AD.","does":"It maintains users, groups, application identities and device identities and performs cloud authentication.","why":"Intune device management depends on identities and group targeting in the Microsoft cloud.","when":"When connecting devices to an organization or controlling Microsoft 365 access.","how":"Set up the tenant and identities, then configure groups, authentication and access policies.","under":"Directory objects are matched to tokens and policies that determine how requests are handled.","verify":"Check Entra user, group, device and sign-in records.","mistake":"Entra joining a computer is not by itself proof that it is enrolled in Intune.","example":"A managed Windows laptop has a device identity in Entra and a separate management record in Intune.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"users"},{"id":"tenant","layer":"identity","name":"Tenant","plain":"A logically isolated organization's cloud environment in a multi-customer service.","does":"It is the boundary containing organization-specific identities, licenses and settings.","why":"Company A's devices, policies and users must not mix with company B's.","when":"When planning administration boundaries or troubleshooting the wrong account/directory.","how":"Confirm the correct tenant before creating a policy, inviting users or granting roles.","under":"Services associate objects with a tenant identifier and enforce scoped access.","verify":"Check tenant name/ID and organization before making a change.","mistake":"A tenant is not an individual laptop, employee, domain, or application.","example":"Northstar's simulated tenant is fictional and cannot control your real Microsoft 365 resources.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"groups"},{"id":"microsoft-entra-registered","layer":"identity","name":"Microsoft Entra registered","plain":"A device has a Microsoft Entra device identity, commonly associated with personal/BYOD use.","does":"It supports work-resource sign-in and device-aware access scenarios.","why":"Personal devices can be recognized without necessarily making work IT the device owner.","when":"When users bring their own supported devices.","how":"Register the device with the work account and decide separately whether Intune enrollment or app-only protection is appropriate.","under":"Registration creates or maintains device identity metadata, but does not automatically install all management capabilities.","verify":"Check the device's Entra join type and its separate Intune enrollment record.","mistake":"Registered does not mean fully managed; registration, join and enrollment are distinct.","example":"A personal Windows laptop registers when the user adds a work account, without necessarily becoming corporate-managed.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"microsoft-entra-joined","layer":"identity","name":"Microsoft Entra joined","plain":"A device is joined to the organization's Microsoft Entra directory as an organizational device.","does":"It enables organizational sign-in and cloud identity integration on supported platforms such as Windows.","why":"Companies want corporate devices to use organizational identities and controls.","when":"When provisioning company-owned Windows devices for employees.","how":"Choose appropriate join method and separately confirm automatic or manual MDM enrollment.","under":"Windows establishes a device identity and sign-in trust with Entra; MDM enrollment, if configured, is a separate management process.","verify":"Check join status, Primary Refresh Token signals where applicable, and Intune management status independently.","mistake":"Entra joined does not guarantee compliant or enrolled in Intune.","example":"Autopilot joins a new company Windows device to Entra and then proceeds with configured Intune enrollment.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"hybrid-joined","layer":"identity","name":"Hybrid joined","plain":"A Windows device joined to on-premises Active Directory and also represented as joined in Microsoft Entra ID.","does":"It bridges existing domain identity to cloud access scenarios.","why":"Some organizations still depend on traditional domain infrastructure.","when":"When migrating older domain-joined environments while maintaining access to on-prem resources.","how":"Plan directory synchronization, network connectivity and join prerequisites carefully.","under":"The device has a domain join relationship and a cloud identity relationship, with additional dependency and timing complexity.","verify":"Check local domain join, Entra registration and device identity state separately.","mistake":"Hybrid join is not the same as a registered BYOD device or cloud-only Entra join.","example":"An inherited Windows workstation continues using on-prem domain policies while obtaining cloud identity.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"static-group","layer":"identity","name":"Static group","plain":"A group whose membership administrators directly add or remove.","does":"It collects users or devices for access or management targeting.","why":"Repeated policy assignments are easier to manage by audience.","when":"When the audience is small, stable, or explicitly curated.","how":"Create the group, select member type, add members and assign policy to the group.","under":"Membership is stored explicitly until changed.","verify":"Compare membership list with the intended pilot or department audience.","mistake":"A static group will not automatically include a newly hired staff member.","example":"IT places five test laptops in a static pilot group before broad deployment.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"groups"},{"id":"dynamic-group","layer":"identity","name":"Dynamic group","plain":"An Entra group whose membership is calculated from a rule about object attributes.","does":"It automatically builds audiences for policies or applications.","why":"At scale, manually adding every new device is error-prone.","when":"When the targeting criteria can be expressed reliably as directory attributes.","how":"Define and validate a membership rule; confirm attribute values and processing results.","under":"Entra evaluates the rule against objects and updates group membership asynchronously.","verify":"Review the group's actual evaluated members, not just the rule text.","mistake":"Dynamic group processing is not identical to Intune assignment filters evaluated during device check-in.","example":"Devices marked with a department value can become members of an accounting group without manual membership changes.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"groups"},{"id":"license","layer":"identity","name":"License","plain":"A subscription entitlement permitting particular Microsoft product capabilities under defined terms.","does":"It enables eligible users or devices to use service features.","why":"A menu being visible does not mean every feature is licensed or supported.","when":"Before using Intune Suite, Windows 365, P2-based features or trial services.","how":"Check exact product entitlement, user/device licensing model and prerequisites without enabling paid options.","under":"Licensing systems associate a subscription with identities or objects and services enforce eligibility.","verify":"Confirm effective license assignment and actual feature access before teaching a live workflow.","mistake":"An administrator role is a permission; a license is a service entitlement.","example":"A test user may have a valid Entra account but not an Intune service entitlement.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"users"},{"id":"rbac","layer":"identity","name":"RBAC","plain":"Role-Based Access Control: permissions organized into job roles rather than granted individually for every action.","does":"It controls which admin tasks an identity can perform and sometimes which objects it can see.","why":"Least privilege reduces accidental or malicious changes.","when":"When assigning help-desk, security or endpoint administrator responsibilities.","how":"Choose the narrowest built-in or custom role and appropriate scope groups/tags.","under":"Access checks evaluate the role's permissions and applicable scope on each action.","verify":"Sign in with a test administrative account to confirm permitted and denied operations.","mistake":"RBAC cannot be replaced with a device-compliance policy; it protects administrative actions.","example":"A first-line technician can initiate a device sync but cannot wipe all corporate laptops.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"roles"},{"id":"microsoft-intune","layer":"management","name":"Microsoft Intune","plain":"Microsoft's cloud-based endpoint management and app protection service.","does":"It stores management policies and assignments, helps deploy apps, and receives device status.","why":"Organizations need consistent settings, security and app lifecycle management across many endpoints.","when":"When administering Windows, Apple or Android devices and protected work applications.","how":"Set up identities, licensing and enrollment, create supported policies, target groups, then monitor results.","under":"The service sends management instructions through supported OS and enrollment channels; devices check in and report outcomes.","verify":"Open a device record, review assignment/evaluation and confirm relevant state on the endpoint.","mistake":"Intune is not the same thing as Entra; directory identity and endpoint management have different jobs.","example":"A security team uses Intune to require encryption and receive compliance outcomes from enrolled laptops.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"devices"},{"id":"mdm","layer":"management","name":"MDM","plain":"Mobile Device Management: OS-level device administration using a vendor-supported management channel.","does":"It can deploy supported device settings, apps and remote actions to enrolled endpoints.","why":"A managed device needs standardized controls beyond an individual app.","when":"When the organization needs platform-level management of a corporate laptop or phone.","how":"Enroll the device, choose platform-supported policies and monitor delivery.","under":"The OS exposes MDM APIs; a device-side management agent or service exchanges management commands and status with Intune.","verify":"Confirm enrollment, last check-in and actual device setting state.","mistake":"MDM cannot automatically control any setting on every OS; platform support matters.","example":"IT sets a Wi-Fi profile for a fleet of enrolled iPads through MDM.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"configuration"},{"id":"mam","layer":"management","name":"MAM","plain":"Mobile Application Management: protection or configuration focused on apps and work data.","does":"It can restrict data movement within supported work apps, including on some devices that are not enrolled.","why":"Personal-device owners may need data protection without full device management.","when":"When using BYOD Outlook or Teams and limiting work-data copying or saving.","how":"Assign app protection to the appropriate users, supported apps and platform, then test behavior.","under":"The app integrates with an approved management SDK or framework and applies per-identity data rules.","verify":"Test a managed work account in a supported app and inspect applied app-protection policy reports.","mistake":"MAM does not mean the entire personal phone is managed.","example":"A company protects Outlook work attachments on an employee's personal iPhone without enrolling the device.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"configuration"},{"id":"enrollment","layer":"management","name":"Enrollment","plain":"The act of establishing an endpoint's management relationship with Intune.","does":"It allows the device to receive supported management commands and report state.","why":"Intune cannot reliably manage an unmanaged device simply because it knows the user.","when":"When onboarding a corporate device or enrolling a permitted personal device.","how":"Select platform-specific enrollment method, satisfy identity and license prerequisites, enroll, then confirm record and check-in.","under":"The endpoint registers a management connection and uses OS-supported channels and credentials or tokens to contact the MDM service.","verify":"Confirm device record, enrollment type, management status, and successful check-in.","mistake":"Device registration or Entra join is not synonymous with Intune MDM enrollment.","example":"A new Windows laptop joins Entra during Autopilot and also enrolls separately in Intune.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"configuration"},{"id":"company-portal","layer":"management","name":"Company Portal","plain":"Microsoft's end-user app or web experience for accessing company apps and device-management information.","does":"It helps users install available apps, initiate enrollment, and resolve some compliance issues.","why":"Users need a self-service route to approved company resources.","when":"When employee-managed enrollment or app self-service is supported.","how":"Install or access the appropriate Company Portal experience and follow supported platform instructions.","under":"The experience connects the signed-in identity to Intune services and presents assigned content.","verify":"Inspect device enrollment or app installation status, not just whether the app opens.","mistake":"Company Portal is not itself proof that the OS is enrolled or that every feature is supported.","example":"An employee opens Company Portal to install an approved business application.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"configuration"},{"id":"policy","layer":"management","name":"Policy","plain":"A defined rule or collection of settings that the organization wants a device, app or access process to follow.","does":"It communicates expected configuration, restriction or evaluation requirements.","why":"Managing hundreds of devices individually is expensive and inconsistent.","when":"When the same requirement should apply to a target audience.","how":"Choose a policy type, configure settings, assign eligible users/devices and observe outcome.","under":"The service stores a policy object; after targeting and device check-in, supported controls evaluate or apply it.","verify":"Check both assignment reporting and observed endpoint state.","mistake":"A policy's existence is not evidence it reached or changed a device.","example":"A security team creates a disk-encryption policy for corporate Windows laptops.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"configuration"},{"id":"configuration-profile","layer":"management","name":"Configuration profile","plain":"A set of settings intended to configure device behavior or OS features.","does":"It can deploy Wi-Fi, firewall, restrictions, browser settings and other platform-supported options.","why":"Administrators want endpoints to start with secure, predictable settings.","when":"When you need to change settings instead of only evaluate whether the device is compliant.","how":"Choose platform/profile type or Settings Catalog, configure settings, assign, synchronize, check results.","under":"Targeted endpoints receive instructions through their OS management channel and report success, failure or conflict.","verify":"Compare profile assignment status with actual setting on an eligible endpoint.","mistake":"Configuration profiles set or manage settings; compliance policies primarily evaluate requirements.","example":"A Windows profile enables firewall settings while a compliance policy separately checks whether the device meets a requirement.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"configuration"},{"id":"settings-catalog","layer":"management","name":"Settings Catalog","plain":"Intune's searchable catalogue of configurable settings for supported platforms.","does":"It groups the settings exposed by Microsoft and platform management providers.","why":"Administrators need precise controls without building a custom XML policy every time.","when":"When creating supported configuration profiles for Windows or Apple platforms.","how":"Choose the right platform, add catalog settings, review conflicts, assign and monitor.","under":"Intune maps selected catalog options to supported management mechanisms and OS settings.","verify":"Verify platform support and the device's reported configuration status.","mistake":"A catalog option's presence does not guarantee every OS edition or version supports it.","example":"IT configures a Windows browser preference using the Settings Catalog.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"configuration"},{"id":"assignment","layer":"management","name":"Assignment","plain":"The link between a policy/app and the users or devices meant to receive it.","does":"It defines who is included or excluded from targeting.","why":"Settings cannot affect all endpoints unless the correct audience is selected.","when":"When rolling out an app, configuration or compliance rule.","how":"Pick the proper user/device groups, exclusions, and applicable filters, then inspect effective targets.","under":"Entra group membership and Intune filtering together determine eligibility before a device processes work.","verify":"Inspect assignments and check one included and one excluded endpoint.","mistake":"Creating a policy without assigning it usually will not deploy it to intended devices.","example":"An app is required for the Accounting group but excluded from an engineering test cohort.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"configuration"},{"id":"assignment-filter","layer":"management","name":"Assignment filter","plain":"Intune-evaluated inclusion or exclusion logic refining a policy or app assignment by supported properties.","does":"It helps target ownership, OS, device type and other supported attributes within broader audiences.","why":"A broad group may contain devices that should not receive the same payload.","when":"When one policy targets a group but only some eligible devices.","how":"Start with an assignment, choose an Include or Exclude filter and validate device property evaluation.","under":"Intune evaluates filters based on device attributes when supported workloads process assignments.","verify":"Review filter results and compare devices inside and outside the filter.","mistake":"An assignment filter is not the same as an Entra dynamic group rule.","example":"Assign to all devices but include only corporate-owned Windows endpoints with an Intune filter.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"configuration"},{"id":"device-check-in","layer":"management","name":"Device check-in","plain":"Communication in which a managed device contacts Intune to request work and report status.","does":"It enables policy delivery, inventory and diagnostic updates.","why":"Cloud management does not require every setting to change instantaneously when an admin clicks Save.","when":"When diagnosing pending assignments or outdated status.","how":"Check network access, device management health, last sync and whether the policy is eligible.","under":"OS management components communicate over supported channels; service queues and OS schedules affect timing.","verify":"Compare last check-in, device-side state and policy results.","mistake":"Clicking Sync requests a check-in; it is not proof every policy succeeded.","example":"A laptop offline overnight will not apply a newly assigned security profile until it communicates again.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"configuration"},{"id":"status-pending-succeeded-failed-or-conflict","layer":"management","name":"Status: pending, succeeded, failed or conflict","plain":"Distinct stages or outcomes in a management workflow.","does":"They describe whether work was received, completed, rejected, or contradicted.","why":"Troubleshooting starts by locating which stage failed.","when":"When an app, configuration profile or compliance state seems wrong.","how":"Inspect assignment eligibility, evaluation timestamps, error information and device logs.","under":"The management service and endpoint each report status, sometimes at different times.","verify":"Look for latest applicable status and evidence from the actual device.","mistake":"Succeeded in a console is not always complete proof of business success; test the actual intended outcome.","example":"A policy is assigned but pending because the target laptop has not checked in.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"configuration"},{"id":"compliance-policy","layer":"security","name":"Compliance policy","plain":"A rule set that evaluates whether a managed endpoint meets defined security or health requirements.","does":"It classifies a device as compliant or noncompliant for supported conditions.","why":"Organizations need an assessment signal before granting sensitive access.","when":"When requiring encryption, a minimum OS, password or security conditions.","how":"Create a compliance policy, assign it, let devices check in and inspect compliance details.","under":"Device or service evaluates required signals and reports state; Entra may consume that state for access decisions.","verify":"Check rule-by-rule compliance and the evaluation timestamp.","mistake":"Compliance policy evaluates; it generally is not the same thing as the configuration that enforces encryption.","example":"A laptop lacking BitLocker reports noncompliant even though it is enrolled correctly.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"conditional-access","layer":"security","name":"Conditional Access","plain":"Microsoft Entra access policies using identity, device and other signals to decide whether access should be allowed, challenged or blocked.","does":"It can require an approved or compliant device before granting cloud-app access.","why":"A correct password alone should not automatically authorize access to sensitive data.","when":"When controlling who can use Exchange Online or other supported resources from specific conditions.","how":"Define user/workload and app scope, conditions and grant controls; safely test in report-only or a pilot first.","under":"Entra evaluates the access request and policy controls using available signals, which can include Intune compliance status.","verify":"Inspect Entra sign-in logs and Conditional Access results alongside Intune compliance.","mistake":"Conditional Access is created in Entra; the Intune node may link to it. It does not configure the device or turn on BitLocker itself.","example":"A user signs in successfully but cloud email is blocked because the device is not compliant.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"access"},{"id":"encryption","layer":"security","name":"Encryption","plain":"Transforming readable information into protected data using cryptographic keys.","does":"It protects stored or transmitted data against unauthorized reading under its threat model.","why":"A stolen laptop or intercepted transmission should not reveal business data easily.","when":"When safeguarding laptop disks, network traffic or app data.","how":"Choose an appropriate encryption mechanism and protect key storage and recovery process.","under":"Mathematical algorithms transform plaintext into ciphertext; authorized keys enable decryption.","verify":"Confirm actual encryption state and key recovery capability, not only a configured setting.","mistake":"Encryption does not fix account compromise or make a device automatically compliant.","example":"A stolen encrypted drive is much harder to read without the necessary key.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"bitlocker","layer":"security","name":"BitLocker","plain":"Microsoft's Windows volume-encryption technology for protecting data at rest.","does":"It encrypts supported drive volumes and works with protectors such as TPM-backed keys and recovery keys.","why":"Lost or stolen Windows laptops create serious data-exposure risk.","when":"When managing corporate Windows endpoints with supported versions and hardware.","how":"Configure disk-encryption policy, test prerequisites and recovery key escrow, sync, then verify encryption.","under":"Windows encrypts the volume and uses key protectors to authorize unlocking during startup or recovery.","verify":"Verify encryption percentage/protection status and secure recovery key location.","mistake":"Saving a policy is not proof the drive is encrypted; TPM alone does not encrypt anything.","example":"An encrypted laptop can require a recovery key after some hardware or boot changes.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"security"},{"id":"bitlocker-recovery-key","layer":"security","name":"BitLocker recovery key","plain":"A recovery secret used to unlock a protected Windows volume when normal protectors cannot.","does":"It allows authorized recovery without permanently losing data.","why":"Devices must remain recoverable after boot or hardware changes.","when":"When planning disk encryption and help-desk support.","how":"Ensure policy includes approved recovery/escrow process, limit who can view keys and audit access.","under":"A recovery secret unlocks the protected volume when normal authentication or protector checks fail.","verify":"Confirm escrow and controlled retrieval in authorized records.","mistake":"A recovery key must be protected; storing it in plain text on the same laptop defeats part of the goal.","example":"IT retrieves a key through an approved workflow after a device enters recovery mode.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"microsoft-defender-for-endpoint","layer":"security","name":"Microsoft Defender for Endpoint","plain":"Microsoft's endpoint threat protection and investigation service.","does":"It can collect security signals, detect malicious behavior and support incident response.","why":"Compliance settings alone cannot investigate an active attack.","when":"When monitoring security events or onboarding organizational devices to EDR.","how":"Configure service integration, onboarding, protection and investigation permissions.","under":"Endpoint sensors and cloud analytics correlate activity and produce detections and recommendations.","verify":"Verify onboarding health, device signals and alert investigation results.","mistake":"Defender for Endpoint is not identical to Microsoft Defender Antivirus or Intune.","example":"An analyst investigates suspicious process execution from a managed Windows laptop.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"antivirus","layer":"security","name":"Antivirus","plain":"Protection that examines files and activity for malware using signatures, heuristics and other defenses.","does":"It can detect, quarantine or block known and suspected malicious code.","why":"Malware threatens data, operations and endpoint integrity.","when":"When configuring baseline endpoint protection.","how":"Set supported protection policy and keep engine/platform/security intelligence current.","under":"Local scanning and behavior signals evaluate objects and processes as they run.","verify":"Confirm protection is on, signatures are current and alerts are reviewed.","mistake":"Antivirus does not replace firewall, patching, EDR or user access control.","example":"A malicious attachment is detected and quarantined on a Windows workstation.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"edr","layer":"security","name":"EDR","plain":"Endpoint Detection and Response: ongoing collection and analysis of endpoint activity for threats.","does":"It helps detect suspicious behavior, investigate it and respond.","why":"Some attacks evade simple file scans and require a time-sequenced investigation.","when":"When an endpoint generates suspicious behavior or an alert.","how":"Onboard endpoints, configure policies and triage alerts with the authorized security team.","under":"Event telemetry is correlated in detection engines; alerts include context such as process trees and device timeline.","verify":"Inspect EDR onboarding and actionable alert evidence.","mistake":"EDR is not the same as antivirus signature scanning.","example":"A security analyst traces unusual PowerShell activity after a phishing report.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"firewall","layer":"security","name":"Firewall","plain":"Traffic-control software or hardware that permits or blocks network communication.","does":"It reduces unwanted network exposure according to rules.","why":"Most endpoints should not accept every inbound network connection.","when":"When defining device network restrictions or investigating blocked apps.","how":"Deploy platform-specific firewall rules, monitor conflicts, and test required business connectivity.","under":"Packets are evaluated against profiles, rules and connection state.","verify":"Check effective firewall policy, event logs and legitimate app connectivity.","mistake":"Disabling all firewall rules is not an acceptable troubleshooting default.","example":"An inbound remote-management port remains blocked except for approved administrators.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"attack-surface-reduction-asr","layer":"security","name":"Attack Surface Reduction (ASR)","plain":"Controls that limit behaviors commonly abused in attacks.","does":"They can block risky process or document actions according to security policy.","why":"Preventing exploitation is often safer than detecting damage after the fact.","when":"When hardening Windows endpoints with supported security capabilities.","how":"Start with audit/pilot where supported, tune exclusions carefully, then enforce and monitor.","under":"Security components inspect process and action context against configured rules.","verify":"Review ASR events and identify legitimate business apps affected by a rule.","mistake":"ASR rules are not the same as a generic network firewall rule.","example":"A malicious macro attempts to launch an unsafe child process and the configured ASR rule blocks it.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"zero-trust","layer":"security","name":"Zero Trust","plain":"A security strategy: verify explicitly, grant least privilege and assume breach rather than trusting network location automatically.","does":"It informs identity, device, app and data controls.","why":"Modern employees work across locations and devices, so inside-office is not equal to trusted.","when":"When designing Conditional Access, device compliance and incident response.","how":"Combine identity controls, device health, least privilege and continuous monitoring.","under":"Each access decision evaluates available signals and enforces scoped access instead of granting blanket trust based on a network perimeter.","verify":"Review whether policies require appropriate identity, device and risk signals.","mistake":"Zero Trust is a strategy, not one switch you turn on in Intune.","example":"A known employee on office Wi-Fi may still need MFA and a compliant device.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"security-baseline","layer":"security","name":"Security baseline","plain":"A curated group of recommended security settings for a supported platform or product.","does":"It offers a consistent starting point for endpoint hardening.","why":"Many teams need tested defaults rather than individually designing every control.","when":"When introducing a new security configuration across an organization.","how":"Review the baseline, adapt it to environment needs, pilot, then monitor conflicts.","under":"Each baseline setting eventually targets an OS capability, which may overlap with other configuration policies.","verify":"Compare effective settings, conflicts and operational impact after assignment.","mistake":"A baseline is not automatically optimal for every company or proof of no security risk.","example":"An organization pilots a Windows security baseline before expanding it to all laptops.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"provisioning","layer":"deployment","name":"Provisioning","plain":"Preparing a device or service so it is ready for an intended user's work.","does":"It supplies identity, settings, apps and access prerequisites.","why":"A new laptop should not require the IT team to manually repeat dozens of tasks.","when":"When an employee receives a new device or Cloud PC.","how":"Define a deployment method, device identity, enrollment, configuration, app installs and readiness checks.","under":"An orchestrated sequence creates or associates device records, processes policies and marks readiness states.","verify":"Confirm user can sign in, required apps work, and policy health is correct.","mistake":"Provisioning is broader than merely connecting a device to Wi-Fi or registering it.","example":"A laptop arrives from a manufacturer and becomes ready for a new hire after enrollment and policy setup.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"autopilot"},{"id":"windows-autopilot","layer":"deployment","name":"Windows Autopilot","plain":"Microsoft's cloud-based Windows deployment and provisioning experience for eligible devices.","does":"It helps transform a new or reset device into a work-ready Windows endpoint using organizational setup.","why":"IT wants consistent zero-touch or low-touch setup at scale.","when":"When shipping organization-owned Windows laptops to users or staging devices.","how":"Register the supported device, assign an Autopilot profile, configure enrollment and ESP, then test the out-of-box experience.","under":"Windows connects during setup, identifies organization-specific provisioning and processes assigned configuration.","verify":"Review Autopilot device assignment, join/enrollment, deployment status and user readiness.","mistake":"Autopilot does not itself install every app or guarantee compliance; those depend on downstream policies and prerequisites.","example":"A new hire opens a sealed laptop and follows the company's branded setup rather than receiving a preconfigured image.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"autopilot"},{"id":"oobe","layer":"deployment","name":"OOBE","plain":"Out-of-Box Experience: the initial operating-system setup a user sees on a new or reset device.","does":"It gathers device language, network and identity information and may trigger organizational provisioning.","why":"User setup must begin with the correct identity and enrollment flow.","when":"During initial Windows Autopilot deployment.","how":"Ensure device can reach required cloud services and is registered and assigned correctly.","under":"Windows setup contacts configured endpoints and, when eligible, invokes organization-defined deployment experience.","verify":"Confirm OOBE displays expected company sign-in and transitions to enrollment.","mistake":"OOBE is not the same as the complete Autopilot process.","example":"A laptop sees the company sign-in page during Windows first-run setup.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"autopilot"},{"id":"enrollment-status-page-esp","layer":"deployment","name":"Enrollment Status Page (ESP)","plain":"A Windows setup experience that communicates and can gate critical enrollment, app and profile installation steps.","does":"It helps avoid giving a user control of an incompletely prepared device.","why":"Business apps and baseline policies may need to be ready before desktop use.","when":"When using Windows Autopilot with supported enrollment scenarios.","how":"Configure the right ESP profile and blocking app requirements, then examine timing and errors.","under":"Windows and Intune report phases such as device preparation, device setup and account setup.","verify":"Inspect which required component delayed or failed, rather than just the final percentage.","mistake":"ESP progress reaching a percentage does not prove every optional application is installed.","example":"An ESP blocks the desktop while a required security agent installs.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"autopilot"},{"id":"windows-update-ring","layer":"deployment","name":"Windows update ring","plain":"An Intune policy controlling Windows update behavior such as deferrals, deadlines and restarts.","does":"It shapes when and how Windows receives and installs updates.","why":"Different user groups need predictable rollout and disruption windows.","when":"When deploying monthly quality updates and managing restart experience.","how":"Pilot update settings, choose supported deadlines and active-hour behavior, assign groups and monitor results.","under":"Windows Update evaluates policies and applicable updates, downloads content and schedules installation/reboots.","verify":"Check OS update history, scan results and Intune update reporting.","mistake":"An update ring is not the same as selecting a particular feature update release.","example":"Pilot laptops receive updates earlier than the broad employee fleet.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"updates"},{"id":"feature-update","layer":"deployment","name":"Feature update","plain":"A Windows update that moves the device to a new feature release or version.","does":"It can change capabilities and support lifecycle beyond monthly fixes.","why":"Organizations plan larger OS changes separately from routine patches.","when":"When preparing a supported Windows version rollout.","how":"Define target version, compatibility prerequisites, staged groups and monitoring.","under":"Windows Update services evaluate eligibility and deploy a release according to policy and safeguards.","verify":"Confirm actual Windows version after restart and review safeguard or failure records.","mistake":"A feature update is not the same thing as a routine quality update.","example":"IT moves compatible Windows devices from one supported release to another after a pilot.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"updates"},{"id":"quality-update","layer":"deployment","name":"Quality update","plain":"A generally monthly Windows update delivering security and reliability fixes.","does":"It reduces known vulnerabilities and corrects issues without necessarily changing the feature release.","why":"Unpatched devices can remain exposed and unstable.","when":"As part of routine maintenance and emergency security response.","how":"Configure update ring or relevant quality-update policy, monitor adoption and restart compliance.","under":"The update client checks applicability, downloads packages, installs them and may require reboot.","verify":"Verify installed update history and device reporting, not only assignment.","mistake":"Quality update and feature update target different types of OS changes.","example":"The security team tracks installation of a critical cumulative update on laptops.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"updates"},{"id":"windows-365-cloud-pc","layer":"deployment","name":"Windows 365 Cloud PC","plain":"A personal or shared Windows desktop hosted in Microsoft's cloud and accessed remotely.","does":"It provides a Windows computing environment without requiring that environment to run on the user's physical device.","why":"Some organizations want managed desktops independent of local hardware.","when":"When provisioning supported cloud desktops for remote or flexible work.","how":"Confirm licensing, provisioning policy, network choice and user assignment before deployment.","under":"Cloud infrastructure creates a virtual Windows endpoint which can be managed through Intune.","verify":"Confirm Cloud PC provisioning state, connectivity, and device policy status.","mistake":"A Cloud PC is a full remote Windows environment, not simply a VPN or a browser tab.","example":"An employee accesses a managed Windows 365 desktop from a personal tablet.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"cloudpc"},{"id":"application","layer":"apps","name":"Application","plain":"Software providing a function to a person or other program.","does":"It processes user tasks and data on the device.","why":"Users need business tools that are functional, up to date and protected.","when":"When deploying, updating, removing or safeguarding apps.","how":"Choose the correct app type, supported platform, deployment method, target group and detection.","under":"Installation changes files/services/registrations; policies may control app configuration or data movement.","verify":"Launch the app as the intended user and inspect deployment status.","mistake":"An app record in Intune does not mean the software is actually installed or usable.","example":"Microsoft 365 Apps must be assigned to the correct devices and install successfully before the employee can work.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/apps/app-management","console":"apps"},{"id":"win32-application","layer":"apps","name":"Win32 application","plain":"Traditional Windows desktop software packaged and deployed using supported Intune app management.","does":"It installs applications outside simple store-based delivery.","why":"Organizations still rely on many custom desktop tools.","when":"When deploying Windows EXE/MSI-based business software.","how":"Package the installer and commands, define detection and requirements, then assign appropriately.","under":"The Intune Management Extension can download, execute and detect the app based on configured logic.","verify":"Check install logs, return codes and detection results after deployment.","mistake":"Successfully running setup is not enough; detection rules must identify the actual installed state.","example":"A finance desktop app is wrapped into an .intunewin package for deployment.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/apps/app-management","console":"apps"},{"id":"intunewin-package","layer":"apps","name":".intunewin package","plain":"A packaged format used to deliver supported Win32 applications through Intune.","does":"It bundles installation source files for deployment processing.","why":"A central service needs the files in a predictable distributable form.","when":"When preparing an EXE or MSI-based Win32 app for Intune.","how":"Use the supported Microsoft content-prep tool with the correct source folder and setup file.","under":"The prepared package is uploaded to Intune and processed by the client during installation.","verify":"Confirm package contains needed files and valid install commands.","mistake":"Packaging does not automatically supply correct detection or uninstall logic.","example":"A vendor's setup files are wrapped before an administrator defines silent installation parameters.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/apps/app-management","console":"apps"},{"id":"detection-rule","layer":"apps","name":"Detection rule","plain":"A test that tells the management service whether a required application is already present.","does":"It helps avoid redundant installs and determines app installation status.","why":"Software deployment must have observable completion evidence.","when":"When deploying Win32 apps with Intune.","how":"Use a reliable file, registry, MSI or script-based check appropriate to the installer.","under":"After executing setup, the client evaluates a rule against actual endpoint state.","verify":"Compare detection result with the app's installed files, version and working behavior.","mistake":"An incorrect detection rule can report failure despite a successful installation or false success despite a broken app.","example":"A rule checks that the installed program's expected executable and version exist.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/apps/app-management","console":"apps"},{"id":"required-versus-available-app","layer":"apps","name":"Required versus available app","plain":"Required assignments install software automatically when eligible; Available assignments let users choose installation in supported self-service experiences.","does":"They determine whether deployment is forced or optional.","why":"Mandatory security tools differ from employee-selectable business apps.","when":"When choosing application assignment intent.","how":"Assign Required for mandated software and Available for appropriate optional apps.","under":"Targeting and intent affect when the client schedules install work or exposes an app for self-service.","verify":"Review assignment intent, device eligibility and actual installation result.","mistake":"Available does not mean installed, and Required does not guarantee success without compatible prerequisites.","example":"Endpoint protection is required, while a graphic design tool might be available on request.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/apps/app-management","console":"apps"},{"id":"dependency-and-supersedence","layer":"apps","name":"Dependency and supersedence","plain":"Dependency means one package relies on another; supersedence describes replacement or updating relationships between versions/apps.","does":"They coordinate installation order and version transition.","why":"Some apps break if prerequisites are missing or old versions remain.","when":"When packaging complex desktop apps or upgrading software fleets.","how":"Define supported prerequisite apps and replacement rules and test upgrade/uninstall behavior.","under":"Intune evaluates related app metadata before or during installation work as supported.","verify":"Validate the final installed versions and expected application behavior.","mistake":"A dependency is not simply an included group assignment, and supersedence is not a Windows update.","example":"A payroll app needs a runtime installed first and later supersedes an older payroll build.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/apps/app-management","console":"apps"},{"id":"app-protection-policy","layer":"apps","name":"App protection policy","plain":"MAM policy controlling corporate-data use in supported applications.","does":"It can restrict sharing, cut/paste, backups, and certain access conditions for work accounts.","why":"Businesses need to protect work data, especially on unmanaged phones.","when":"When allowing BYOD access without full-device MDM enrollment.","how":"Target the correct user/app/platform and test work-data actions.","under":"Supported app code or SDK enforces controls for the organizational identity.","verify":"Try a protected work-account action and consult app protection monitoring.","mistake":"App protection is different from an app installation policy and may work without device enrollment.","example":"An employee can read work email but cannot save corporate attachments into an unmanaged personal app.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/apps/app-management","console":"protection"},{"id":"app-configuration-policy","layer":"apps","name":"App configuration policy","plain":"A policy supplying managed application settings such as account preconfiguration.","does":"It can reduce manual setup and enforce supported app preferences.","why":"A large fleet should not require every employee to type the same server parameters.","when":"When supported apps need organization-specific settings.","how":"Choose managed device or managed app delivery context, supported configuration keys, assignment and validation.","under":"Management services pass approved key-value settings through MDM or supported managed-app channels.","verify":"Confirm the running app receives and respects the intended values.","mistake":"App configuration and app protection solve different problems: setup versus work-data safeguards.","example":"Outlook receives the correct work account configuration automatically.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/apps/app-management","console":"apps"},{"id":"byod","layer":"apps","name":"BYOD","plain":"Bring Your Own Device: an employee uses a personally owned endpoint for work.","does":"It enables work access from non-company hardware with appropriate data boundaries.","why":"Device ownership changes which controls are acceptable and proportionate.","when":"When employees want supported email, Teams or other work access on personal phones.","how":"Decide if app protection alone suffices or if opt-in enrollment is necessary and allowed.","under":"Identity and app controls can protect company data while preserving private-device ownership boundaries.","verify":"Check the device-ownership and enrollment model with user notice and access policy.","mistake":"BYOD does not mean the organization should automatically get full device wipe rights.","example":"A personal Android phone accesses managed work apps under MAM protections.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/apps/app-management","console":"apps"},{"id":"powershell","layer":"operations","name":"PowerShell","plain":"A command shell and scripting language used to automate administrative tasks.","does":"It can query systems, configure supported settings and generate reports.","why":"Repetitive manual steps are slow, inconsistent and hard to audit.","when":"When collecting endpoint diagnostics or automating approved maintenance.","how":"Learn cmdlets, parameters, output, scope and error handling; test non-destructive commands before changes.","under":"A PowerShell pipeline passes structured objects to commands, while scripts perform ordered operations in an authorized session.","verify":"Inspect command output, exit codes and resulting system state.","mistake":"A script returning exit code 0 does not automatically prove the intended outcome on every device.","example":"An admin collects free-disk information from test laptops before a major update.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"scripts"},{"id":"microsoft-graph-api","layer":"operations","name":"Microsoft Graph API","plain":"Microsoft's REST API for accessing supported Microsoft cloud resources through authorized calls.","does":"It allows scripts and apps to read or modify supported cloud objects programmatically.","why":"At scale, automation needs an authenticated interface rather than manual portal clicks.","when":"When automating reports, inventory queries or safe approved management changes.","how":"Register an application or use delegated access, grant the least necessary permissions, send authenticated API requests and handle paging/errors.","under":"The client sends HTTPS requests with an access token; Graph validates permissions and routes to the underlying service.","verify":"Check HTTP status, returned object, auditing and actual backend state.","mistake":"Graph API access requires permissions and does not bypass Intune licensing or role controls.","example":"A script lists managed devices for an authorized monthly compliance report.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"scripts"},{"id":"api","layer":"operations","name":"API","plain":"Application Programming Interface: a defined way for software components to request actions or data.","does":"It connects independently developed systems without manual human clicks.","why":"IT tools must communicate reliably with documented contracts.","when":"When automating device inventory or integrating monitoring.","how":"Read documentation, confirm auth, request format, responses, pagination and rate limits.","under":"A client sends an operation and parameters; a service processes the request and returns a response or error.","verify":"Inspect response status and returned fields before assuming success.","mistake":"API means interface, not necessarily a public internet URL or permission to change everything.","example":"A dashboard uses an API to request the list of enrolled laptops.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"reports"},{"id":"access-token","layer":"operations","name":"Access token","plain":"A credential-like signed or opaque artifact used to authorize access to a particular resource.","does":"It provides evidence of a successful authentication and authorized scopes or roles for a period of time.","why":"Applications need permissioned access without constantly forwarding user passwords.","when":"When using Graph or signing into cloud applications.","how":"Obtain tokens through the approved identity flow, use correct scope/audience and keep tokens secret.","under":"The resource validates expiry, issuer, audience and permissions before honoring a request.","verify":"Inspect token claims only in safe test contexts and review service error logs.","mistake":"A token is not a password and may be unusable for another API with a different audience.","example":"A Graph request fails with 403 because the token lacks the required permission.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"reports"},{"id":"kql","layer":"operations","name":"KQL","plain":"Kusto Query Language: a language used to filter, aggregate and analyze structured telemetry where supported.","does":"It helps investigators find patterns in logs and device data.","why":"Manual scanning of thousands of events is unreliable.","when":"When using supported security queries, reporting or device-query experiences.","how":"Identify the dataset, available columns and permissions; write and test read-only queries.","under":"A query engine filters rows, projects columns, groups data and computes results.","verify":"Validate field names and result timestamps against source documentation.","mistake":"KQL is not PowerShell and not every Intune table or workspace supports the same queries.","example":"An analyst filters device events to identify endpoints with repeated update failures.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"reports"},{"id":"logs-and-telemetry","layer":"operations","name":"Logs and telemetry","plain":"Time-stamped records or measurements of system behavior, errors and state.","does":"They provide evidence of what happened and when.","why":"The administrator's intention is not the same as the endpoint's actual experience.","when":"When enrollment, deployment, security or update behavior is unexpected.","how":"Gather the relevant device, app, enrollment and service logs; correlate by time and ID.","under":"Independent components emit events as they process operations; clocks and reporting delays can differ.","verify":"Find a specific status code, correlated event and observed impact.","mistake":"A green dashboard tile is not a substitute for a relevant device log in a complex failure.","example":"An app install reports failed, and logs reveal a missing prerequisite rather than bad assignment.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"reports"},{"id":"remediation","layer":"operations","name":"Remediation","plain":"A scripted detection-and-fix process for an identified endpoint condition.","does":"It can automatically identify an issue and take a controlled corrective step.","why":"Repeated known configuration drift can be repaired without repetitive manual tickets.","when":"When a problem has a safe, testable and reversible fix.","how":"Define detection criteria, write scoped remediation script, pilot and monitor output and risks.","under":"The detection stage returns a result that can cause remediation to run; the client records exit code and output.","verify":"Check detection status and independently verify the issue was resolved.","mistake":"A remediation script should not be used to blindly alter thousands of devices without review.","example":"A script finds a stopped approved service and restarts it after verifying prerequisites.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"scripts"},{"id":"endpoint-analytics","layer":"operations","name":"Endpoint Analytics","plain":"Microsoft reporting and insights that help evaluate endpoint experience and issues.","does":"It summarizes signals like startup performance or anomalies in supported capabilities.","why":"IT needs to prioritize user-impacting problems across many devices.","when":"When investigating sluggish deployments or deciding where to optimize endpoints.","how":"Ensure prerequisites and licensing, review insights, choose a pilot change and compare results.","under":"Devices send supported metrics that are processed into scores and recommendations.","verify":"Correlate an insight with actual endpoint conditions and before/after measurements.","mistake":"An analytics score is not itself a compliance policy or security verdict.","example":"A startup-performance insight identifies a recurring app issue across the laptop fleet.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"reports"},{"id":"remote-action-sync-retire-and-wipe","layer":"operations","name":"Remote action: sync, retire and wipe","plain":"Administrative actions that request a device check-in, remove organizational management/data, or reset data according to platform and action.","does":"They support lifecycle changes and incident response.","why":"IT must respond to lost devices, reassignment and troubleshooting.","when":"When a device is out of date, returned, decommissioned or compromised.","how":"Identify the correct device, understand the exact platform-specific consequence, authorize and verify the action.","under":"Intune queues supported commands; the device executes them when reachable, then reports outcomes.","verify":"Inspect execution status and resulting enrollment/data state.","mistake":"Sync is a refresh request; retire is not identical to wipe; wipe can be destructive.","example":"IT retires a personally owned device to remove managed company data without casually erasing personal photos.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"devices"},{"id":"reporting-and-evidence","layer":"operations","name":"Reporting and evidence","plain":"Structured records showing device, app, policy or security outcomes over time.","does":"They help administrators prove whether controls operated as intended.","why":"A change cannot be trusted just because a Save button was clicked.","when":"When auditing deployment results or explaining an incident.","how":"Define the expected result, locate source reports, sample endpoints and timestamp the findings.","under":"Different services collect signals at different intervals, so reporting can lag actual events.","verify":"Compare portal status, device state and event logs for a consistent conclusion.","mistake":"Counts are not always live and don't establish causality without underlying evidence.","example":"A rollout is considered successful only after eligible laptops show the required app working.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"reports"},{"id":"bit","layer":"hardware","name":"Bit","plain":"The smallest conventional unit of digital information, having one of two values: 0 or 1.","does":"Bits represent choices in logic and build larger stored or transmitted data.","why":"Every app, setting and network packet ultimately uses encoded digital information.","when":"When explaining how computers store, transmit or secure information.","how":"Think of a bit as one binary switch, then combine bits into larger values.","under":"Electrical or magnetic physical states encode logical 0 and 1; actual hardware uses complex circuits and error handling.","verify":"View the number of bits in a data representation and how they combine into bytes.","mistake":"Bit is not byte; eight bits normally make one byte.","example":"A short text message is encoded as many bits before a network card transmits it.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"byte","layer":"hardware","name":"Byte","plain":"A unit of digital data consisting of eight bits.","does":"It helps measure file, memory and storage sizes.","why":"Device capacity and update requirements need consistent units.","when":"When comparing RAM, disks, download packages, or application requirements.","how":"Read capacity in bytes and appropriate larger units, remembering that marketing GB and binary GiB can differ.","under":"Eight binary digits form one byte capable of representing 256 values.","verify":"Compare file size or free space with required deployment capacity.","mistake":"A byte is not a single character in every text encoding; some characters require multiple bytes.","example":"An app installer may require several gigabytes of free space, far more than a small settings file.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"binary-code","layer":"hardware","name":"Binary code","plain":"Instructions and data represented using two-state digital values.","does":"It provides the foundation on which processors and software exchange machine-readable information.","why":"Computers need precise representations that electronic circuits can process.","when":"When understanding how source code eventually becomes executable instructions.","how":"Distinguish human-readable source text from machine-level instructions and raw data.","under":"Compilers and interpreters translate or execute programs through OS and CPU mechanisms.","verify":"Check a file's format or architecture when diagnosing incompatibility.","mistake":"Binary does not mean 'secure' or 'encrypted'; plain binary data can be readable.","example":"A Windows application built for one processor architecture may be incompatible with another.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"file-system","layer":"hardware","name":"File system","plain":"The OS-managed structure that organizes files and directories on storage.","does":"It tracks names, metadata, permissions and locations of stored data.","why":"Apps and administrators need reliable ways to locate and protect files.","when":"When troubleshooting app installation, disk usage or encryption and access issues.","how":"Check paths, permissions, available capacity and file-system health.","under":"The OS maps logical files to storage blocks and enforces supported access rules.","verify":"Confirm expected files exist and permissions allow the intended action.","mistake":"A file path is not the same as the application installation status in Intune.","example":"A Win32 detection rule checks a particular file's presence and version.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"process-and-thread","layer":"hardware","name":"Process and thread","plain":"A process is a running program instance; a thread is a sequence of execution within a process.","does":"They perform application and service work on the processor.","why":"Application errors often involve processes, permissions, threads or resource pressure.","when":"When reviewing Task Manager, security alerts or installation logs.","how":"Identify the process, executable, account context and associated child processes.","under":"The OS schedules threads on CPU cores and isolates processes through memory and security boundaries.","verify":"Inspect process identifiers and logs, then relate them to app behavior.","mistake":"A process is running work, while an executable file is stored program content.","example":"EDR investigates a suspicious child process launched by an email attachment.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/platform-guide-windows","console":"devices"},{"id":"router","layer":"network","name":"Router","plain":"A device or service that forwards packets between different networks.","does":"It connects a local device's network to remote destinations.","why":"Traffic to the internet normally needs a path beyond the local subnet.","when":"When a laptop sees the printer but cannot reach Microsoft services.","how":"Verify its default gateway, routing path and network security rules.","under":"The router examines destination IP information and chooses a next hop.","verify":"Check reachability to gateway and approved remote network destinations.","mistake":"A router is not a DNS server by definition, even when one box offers both functions.","example":"A laptop has Wi-Fi but lacks a usable gateway route to the internet.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"http-request-and-response","layer":"network","name":"HTTP request and response","plain":"A structured message sent by a client to a web service and the corresponding service reply.","does":"It carries requests for pages or API operations, plus status and content.","why":"Cloud services and management APIs depend on predictable request and response behavior.","when":"When diagnosing whether a portal or API call failed.","how":"Identify the method, URL, authentication, request body and returned status.","under":"The client sends headers and optional body; the server processes the request and returns a status code and content.","verify":"Inspect whether the response was 200, 401, 403, 404, or another meaningful code.","mistake":"A successful HTTP connection does not mean authorization or the requested business change succeeded.","example":"A Graph request returns 403 because the identity lacks a required permission.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"bandwidth-and-latency","layer":"network","name":"Bandwidth and latency","plain":"Bandwidth describes how much data a link can carry in a period; latency describes delay before information arrives.","does":"They influence download speed and interactive responsiveness.","why":"Enrollment or app delivery can fail or feel slow when network quality is poor.","when":"When large applications download slowly or remote sessions lag.","how":"Compare link performance, round-trip time, packet loss and content size.","under":"Throughput depends on available capacity, contention and transport behavior; latency accumulates across links and services.","verify":"Measure controlled test performance and compare it with application logs.","mistake":"High advertised bandwidth does not always mean low latency or no packet loss.","example":"A Cloud PC feels sluggish over a long-delay connection even though the nominal bandwidth is high.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/deployment-guide-enrollment","console":"enrollment"},{"id":"directory-object","layer":"identity","name":"Directory object","plain":"A structured record representing a user, device, group or application in a directory service.","does":"It stores identities and attributes needed for lookup and access decisions.","why":"Systems need durable references rather than just display names.","when":"When examining user/device properties or group membership.","how":"Find the correct object ID and inspect attributes before changing access.","under":"The directory stores objects by identifiers; other services refer to these identifiers for targeting.","verify":"Confirm object ID, type, state and relevant audit events.","mistake":"Display names can change or duplicate; object IDs distinguish records.","example":"Two employees may share a name but have different Entra user object IDs.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"groups"},{"id":"attribute","layer":"identity","name":"Attribute","plain":"A named property of an object, such as department, operating system or ownership.","does":"It provides data for search, targeting, reports and decisions.","why":"Dynamic rules need machine-readable criteria rather than guessing.","when":"When setting up groups, assignment filters or device inventory.","how":"Confirm the correct attribute exists and contains an accurate value before writing a rule.","under":"Rule engines compare stored or reported property values to their configured predicates.","verify":"Check the actual object property and which source updates it.","mistake":"An attribute that is blank or stale can make a correct-looking rule target the wrong population.","example":"A department field determines whether a user appears in a dynamic Accounting group.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"groups"},{"id":"token-claim","layer":"identity","name":"Token claim","plain":"A fact carried inside a security token, such as issuer, audience, identity, time or permissions.","does":"It helps a resource evaluate which requester and grant are represented by a token.","why":"A web service must verify both authenticity and intended use of a token.","when":"When diagnosing authentication and Microsoft Graph permission issues.","how":"Validate the issuer, audience, expiration and relevant scopes/roles through authorized diagnostics.","under":"The identity provider issues a token; the resource verifies its signature or validation result and claims.","verify":"Inspect protected request errors and permitted token metadata, not raw production secrets in chat.","mistake":"A token may be valid but rejected because its audience is for a different resource.","example":"A Graph API call fails because the access token lacks the expected scope.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview","console":"groups"},{"id":"cryptographic-key","layer":"security","name":"Cryptographic key","plain":"A secret or public value used by a cryptographic algorithm for protected operations.","does":"It controls encryption/decryption or signature verification depending on the algorithm.","why":"Encryption without properly protected key material does not provide dependable security.","when":"When configuring TLS certificates, BitLocker recovery, or identity protection.","how":"Decide where keys are generated, protected, backed up, rotated and recovered.","under":"Algorithms use key material and data to compute ciphertext, authentication tags or signatures.","verify":"Verify key storage, access control, escrow and recovery procedures.","mistake":"A key is not necessarily the same as a password or certificate; a certificate usually binds a public key to identity.","example":"TPM-backed key protection helps secure the material used to unlock a BitLocker volume.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"plaintext-and-ciphertext","layer":"security","name":"Plaintext and ciphertext","plain":"Plaintext is readable input; ciphertext is the encoded result produced by encryption.","does":"They describe data before and after a cryptographic transformation.","why":"Data stolen from a disk should not be readable without authorized decryption.","when":"When explaining the purpose and limits of disk or network encryption.","how":"Identify which data is protected and where decryption occurs.","under":"An algorithm combines plaintext with a key to create ciphertext and uses authorized key material to recover plaintext.","verify":"Confirm the actual disk or transport encryption state and key custody.","mistake":"Ciphertext alone does not prove the correct keys are protected or that endpoint malware is prevented.","example":"A thief copies an encrypted laptop volume but cannot interpret its file contents without the required key.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"hash","layer":"security","name":"Hash","plain":"A fixed-length representation calculated from data by a hashing algorithm.","does":"It can support integrity checks, indexing and some cryptographic schemes.","why":"Administrators need to detect altered files or verify download integrity.","when":"When validating deployment packages or investigating tampering.","how":"Compute a cryptographic hash for the expected file and compare it with a trusted value.","under":"A function maps input bytes to a digest; a change in input usually changes its output.","verify":"Recalculate and compare the digest from a trusted reference.","mistake":"Hashing is not reversible encryption, and a hash by itself does not establish who created a file.","example":"Two admins compare SHA-256 digests for an approved application package.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/fundamentals/zero-trust-deployment","console":"compliance"},{"id":"command-and-script","layer":"operations","name":"Command and script","plain":"A command requests one action; a script combines commands and decisions into repeatable logic.","does":"They automate collection or configuration work.","why":"Reliable operations require reproducible actions and error handling.","when":"When replacing repetitive help-desk tasks with controlled automation.","how":"Start with read-only commands, review scope, then build tests, guards, logs and rollback into a script.","under":"A shell parses commands and invokes programs or language runtime functions with permissions.","verify":"Review output, logs and final device state after execution.","mistake":"Script text is not a policy and a script completing is not proof it solved the issue.","example":"A script queries the BitLocker status of pilot devices before any change is made.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"reports"},{"id":"json","layer":"operations","name":"JSON","plain":"JavaScript Object Notation: a text format for structured data using objects, arrays, strings, numbers and booleans.","does":"It helps applications and APIs exchange settings and results in a predictable shape.","why":"Automation needs clearly defined machine-readable properties.","when":"When using Microsoft Graph, custom compliance or configuration payloads.","how":"Read the expected schema, property names, types and valid values before sending data.","under":"A parser turns JSON text into structured values for a program to validate and use.","verify":"Validate syntax and required keys, then inspect the service's response.","mistake":"A JSON property name mismatch may break custom compliance even when the script returns useful data.","example":"A compliance script returns a field named EncryptionState but a rules file expects another exact name.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"reports"},{"id":"error-code-and-status","layer":"operations","name":"Error code and status","plain":"A machine-readable indication of the result or failure of an operation.","does":"It gives an investigator a starting point for finding the failed stage.","why":"Troubleshooting by guesswork can cause unnecessary or harmful changes.","when":"When an app, API or MDM workflow reports failed, blocked or pending.","how":"Record the exact code, timestamp, action context and related logs before searching documented causes.","under":"Independent components return statuses based on their own contracts and phases.","verify":"Correlate the code with device/service logs and a confirmed corrective action.","mistake":"A 'Succeeded' HTTP response or process exit does not always prove the end user's task works.","example":"A package installer returns success but the app's detection rule still fails.","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/intune/intune-service/fundamentals/manage-devices","console":"reports"}];